{
  "$schema": "./node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "charcha",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-07-23",
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  },
  "upload_source_maps": true,

  // The embed ships as a static asset rather than from a Worker route (#5).
  // "Requests to static assets are free and unlimited. Requests to the Worker script
  // [...] are billed according to Workers pricing"
  // (https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/billing-and-limitations/,
  // checked 2026-07-24). After the read-path work in #20 it is the request count and
  // not the database that bounds a site on the free tier, so serving embed.js from
  // the Worker would make every page view cost two requests instead of one and halve
  // a site's ceiling from ~100k to ~50k page views a day.
  //
  // Every default here is load-bearing and deliberately left unset:
  //   - `run_worker_first` defaults to false. Setting it — for any pattern covering
  //     embed.js — makes those requests invoke and bill the Worker, which is exactly
  //     the cost this binding exists to avoid.
  //   - `not_found_handling` defaults to "none". Cloudflare serves a matching asset
  //     without invoking the Worker and otherwise falls through to it, which is what
  //     keeps GET/POST/OPTIONS /comments and /health reaching Hono. The
  //     "single-page-application" and "404-page" modes send navigation requests
  //     straight to asset serving instead, and would break the read path.
  // https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/routing/worker-script/
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./public"
  },

  // public/embed.js is a build output and is gitignored, so it has to be built
  // before the directory above is uploaded. Wrangler runs this before `deploy` and
  // watches it during `dev`.
  //
  // **Workers Builds does not honour this** — "Currently, Workers Builds does not
  // honor the configurations set in Custom Builds within your Wrangler configuration
  // file" (https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/ci-cd/builds/configuration/,
  // checked 2026-07-24). What covers the Deploy button instead is the `build` script
  // in package.json, which the button detects and pre-populates
  // (https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/deploy-buttons/). Both exist
  // because they cover different paths, not by duplication.
  "build": {
    "command": "pnpm build"
  },

  // Daily retention janitor: nulls comments.ip_hash past the configured window so
  // the IP-derived identifier does not live in the database forever (#19). One
  // trigger, well inside the Free plan's 5-per-account limit. The window itself is
  // the `ip_hash_retention_days` setting, not this schedule.
  "triggers": {
    "crons": ["0 4 * * *"]
  },

  // The login throttle (#12). `POST /admin/api/session` is a public,
  // unauthenticated endpoint guarding the one credential that can delete every
  // comment on the site, so it needs a brute-force bound — and it must not be #8's
  // rate-limit layer. That one counts rows in `comments` by `ip_hash`, and a login
  // attempt writes no comment row; a D1-backed attempt counter would mean one write
  // per failed attempt, so a brute-forcer at 10 req/s spends 864k writes/day against
  // a 100k/day budget and takes commenting down site-wide. This binding stores
  // nothing and costs nothing.
  //
  // "Rate limits that you define and enforce in your Worker are local to the
  // Cloudflare location that your Worker runs in" and the counters are "permissive,
  // eventually consistent, and intentionally designed to not be used as an accurate
  // accounting system" — which is the right trade for a login throttle and the wrong
  // one for a quota. `period` must be 10 or 60; there is no other value.
  // https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/bindings/rate-limit/
  // GA since 2025-09-19: https://developers.cloudflare.com/changelog/2025-09-19-ratelimit-workers-ga/
  // Both checked 2026-07-25.
  //
  // **Free-plan availability is undocumented.** The binding is GA and nothing says
  // it is plan-gated, but nothing says it is not — and it is not in the Deploy
  // button's list of provisioned resources either (that list is KV, D1, R2,
  // Hyperdrive, Vectorize, Secrets Store, Durable Objects, Workers AI, Queues,
  // checked 2026-07-25). It needs no provisioning, being declared entirely here,
  // but a plan gate would fail `wrangler deploy` for every one-click deployer. See
  // the owner check on #12.
  "ratelimits": [
    {
      "name": "LOGIN_RATE_LIMITER",
      "namespace_id": "1001",
      "simple": {
        // Ten attempts a minute per client. A person mistyping their own password
        // does not reach it; a script is stopped at three orders of magnitude
        // below what guessing a real secret would need.
        "limit": 10,
        "period": 60
      }
    }
  ],

  // The Deploy to Cloudflare button reads this file to decide what to provision,
  // and rewrites `database_id` with the ID of the database it creates. The value
  // below is a placeholder that exists only so the binding is well-formed before
  // any deployment has happened.
  "d1_databases": [
    {
      "binding": "DB",
      "database_name": "charcha",
      "database_id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000"
    }
  ]
}
